WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.590
Okay.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:09.800
Bayer, the massive pharmaceutical manufacturer
has bailed out on two major Fox News.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.950
Opinion shows, uh, that's me pretending that
there's actually a significant difference

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:19.260
between Fox opinion and Fox News reporting
moments and programs and hosts.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.180
A Bayer has pulled advertising from Tucker
Carlson's show and from Laura Ingram's show.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.859
Now both of these shows you might know have
already had serious advertising issues because

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:34.660
of the shows are toxic and Bayer has been
a major advertiser.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:41.230
Bayer was Tucker's fifth largest advertiser
and Laura Ingram, second largest advertiser.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.969
And the reason for the cancellation is being
reported as white nationalist rhetoric on

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:54.360
Tucker Carlson's show, uh, and Laura Ingram
defending a white supremacist named Paul Nealon

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.850
on her program.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:01.370
And interestingly, Bayer has actually a little
while ago, started reducing and pulling ads

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.539
off of these shows.

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:06.439
Now it's being formally reported based on
contacts with Bayer.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:10.799
So what I want to do here is cut through all
of the bs for you because even within our

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.990
audience, the idea of an advertiser boycott
is controversial.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.840
Sometimes when we talk about advertiser boycotts,
people will make so called free speech arguments

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.749
against the ad boycotts.

00:01:21.749 --> 00:01:27.969
Even though you can't argue against the reality
that calling for a boycott is a form of speech.

00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:35.310
A company deciding where to advertise using
whatever system they want to use to figure

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.289
out where to advertise is a form of speech.

00:01:38.289 --> 00:01:43.439
So there's this weird thing about criticizing
add boycotts and demands for ad boycotts on

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:49.340
the basis of free speech because it is a form
of speech in and of itself to say to a company,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.729
please don't advertise on this show.

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:57.270
There are some times sort of weird business
regulation arguments that people will make

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:02.159
against ad boycotts, although I don't even
understand those in this context, so I don't

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:03.159
even know how to refute them.

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:05.049
They just don't make any sense to me.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:06.079
Here's the bottom line.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:07.729
As I tweeted yesterday.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:13.579
If you don't like the fact that advertiser
boycotts can negatively impact shows that

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:17.709
you watch shows that you listen to, shows
that you like.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:22.730
If you don't want advertiser cancellations
to be a threat to programs that you think

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:28.700
are good programs, stop making media reliant
on ad dollars.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:36.069
What I mean by this is if we didn't have a
system where shows depended on advertising

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:43.549
money, then the ad boycott would lose its
place as a threat to the existence of programming.

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:46.859
This is why I've been trying to grow our direct
membership for years.

00:02:46.859 --> 00:02:50.930
I know that we joke about how much I talk
about membership or whatever.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:57.140
Every dollar that we get from membership is
a dollar of what I would call immunity from

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:01.420
advertisers, from Youtube, demonetization,
which we'll talk about later because that's

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.420
happening.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.890
Again, Facebook, demonetization, this couldn't
be more obvious one way or another.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.360
You are paying for the show.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.360
Okay?

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.700
You either watch an ad and pay with your attention
and then the advertiser is the one that actually

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.910
makes the financial transfer of dollars directly
or you pay the producer directly through a

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:25.299
membership through a pledge drive on NPR or
PBS through a cable subscription for some

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:26.299
channels.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:33.400
If we had more direct financial support when
youtube app apocalypse hit in 2017 it wouldn't

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.310
have mattered if our level of financial support,
uh, d was decimated from youtube because we

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.160
would have that direct support.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.019
In the same way we have advertisers, someone
could, if they wanted to go to our advertisers

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:51.110
and say, I won't support you if you continue
to advertise with David Pakman.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:56.150
Now I'm guessing most advertisers would say,
David Pakman hasn't said anything discriminatory

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.279
or racist or xenophobic.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:03.779
We don't know why we would pull our ads from
David Pakman, but that would be a threat that

00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:08.290
disappears if our level of direct financial
support goes up.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.840
The legal side of this is super clear, like
I'm not making a partisan argument.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.870
Advertising choices are a form of speech period.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:22.759
Lobbying companies not to advertise or to
advertise on whatever show is a form of speech.

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:26.520
It's protected 100% it's 100% fine.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.840
We can debate whether an ad boycott is a good
strategy.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.740
I think it's great when activism has an impact,
right?

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.639
In the real world, it's harder and harder
for an individual's voice to matter.

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:44.060
We know that the, the demands or the desires
of rich people are way more likely to become

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:49.280
law or policy than the demands of poor people
and middle class people, the demands or the

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.479
ideas or the, the desires of big corporations
are way more likely to be taken seriously

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:59.520
by our elected officials than those of just
your individual Joe or Sally or whoever.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.740
So in a world where it's harder and harder
for an individual to have their voice heard,

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:08.509
I think it's great when activism has an impact
and it has had an impact through ad boycotts.

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:16.879
But I agree that there is a real problem when
media outlets are so, uh, over a barrel by

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:18.349
advertisers.

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:23.169
And the way to fix that is support the shows
that you like.

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:28.180
If you want them to cover what they want to
cover, not be threatened by the loss of ad

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.180
dollars.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.580
If they have a membership program, sign up.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:32.629
Fox is different.

00:05:32.629 --> 00:05:37.569
Number one, they don't actually even really
